35,2,1,talk,Servas,,"My findings reveal a poignant truth: while we have an abundance of digital architects, the seasoned hands for phenotyping have vanished into industry. We must be cautious not to fund a project that lacks the actual hands to execute it.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Sakura,,"Ooh, I found some secrets! Computing is free, which is awesome, but animal costs have shot up! Also, shipping is getting pricier while travel money is stuck. Some projects might be way more expensive than they look!","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I found that projects focusing on broad catalogues tend to provide lasting value, while those chasing specific mechanisms often fail. Also, projects relying on live breeding colonies are much more likely to overrun their schedules.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Takumi,,"It's a mess. The spectroscopy suite was dead for eleven weeks because of a lapsed contract, and field sites are getting wiped out by floods and fire. We can't trust projects that rely on fragile equipment or unstable sites.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Minako,,"The infrastructure is a mixed bag. The national repository is now efficient, and animal facilities are stable with veterinary cover. However, the glasshouses are completely booked. We cannot support any project requiring immediate plant growth space.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Servas,,"It is a delicate balance. @Minako, you mention the glasshouses are full, yet my records show the crew there is at full strength. We have the people, but not the space. This is a curious contradiction.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Sakura,,"@Minako, that's a big problem! If the glasshouses are full, any plant project is just a dream. But wait, I saw the ministry is still paying for glasshouse space! Why is it full if it's subsidized?","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I'm not sure about the glasshouses, but maybe we should be careful about the biological risks in general. If breeding colonies cause so many delays, maybe a more data-driven project is safer?","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Daisuke, you're right. Between the field sites burning down and the equipment just sitting there because of paperwork, biological projects are a gamble. We need something that won't just stop the moment a pipe leaks.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Minako,,"@Sakura, subsidies do not create physical space. The demand simply exceeds the capacity. @Daisuke, while data-driven projects are safer, we must ensure the computing infrastructure can handle the load without creating new bottlenecks.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Sakura"",""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Servas,,"We must remember that data is but a reflection of reality. If we pivot entirely to computational work, we risk ignoring the physical world. However, we cannot ignore that our phenotyping expertise has dwindled.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Sakura,,"But if computing is free, that's a huge win! We can do a lot of data work without worrying about the budget. Let's find a project that's smart with its money and doesn't need a glasshouse!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I agree with Sakura. If we can find a project that focuses on a broad catalogue or data analysis, it might be more reliable. I'm just worried about picking something that stalls again.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Sakura""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Takumi,,"@Daisuke, exactly. We can't afford another project that stalls because of some ""unforeseen"" disaster. If a proposal depends on field sites or specialized gear that's barely maintained, it's a non-starter for me. We need stability.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Minako,,"Stability is indeed the priority. Given the glasshouse shortage and the instability of field sites, we should prioritize candidates whose methodologies are computationally heavy or rely on the now-efficient national repository and stable animal facilities.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Servas,,Let us hold these truths in our hearts as we move forward. We seek a project that harmonizes ambition with the reality of our current strengths—stable animal facilities and digital capacity—while respecting the absence of phenotyping hands.,"{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Sakura,,"I'm totally on board! Let's look for a project that's a ""budget superstar""—something that uses that free computing and avoids those scary animal costs and full glasshouses. I can't wait to see the candidates!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I hope we can find a project that doesn't just look good on paper. If we prioritize something that builds a lasting resource, maybe we can finally avoid the cycle of projects that just... disappear.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Takumi,,"I'm done with the guesswork. We've seen the failures: broken gear, burnt fields, and missing staff. Whoever we pick better have a plan that doesn't collapse the second something goes wrong. Let's see the candidates.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Minako,,"We have a clear set of constraints. We must avoid projects dependent on glasshouses or unstable field sites, and be wary of those requiring rare phenotyping expertise. I am ready to evaluate the candidates against these facts.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
